const title = "Whirlpool Dishwasher Not Drying? Causes & Fix — Same Day";
const description = `Whirlpool dishwasher leaving dishes wet? Same-day LA. Heating element (F7E1), high-limit thermostat, Heated Dry option, fan. Also KitchenAid/Maytag. BHGS #A49573. $89 dx.`;

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "My Whirlpool dishwasher isn't drying — is it the heating element?",
    a: "On a Whirlpool it's a real possibility, because most Whirlpool-family dishwashers dry with an exposed heating element at the tub floor (heated dry), not the condensation method Bosch uses. A burned-out element leaves dishes wet, and usually the wash water feels under-heated too. Whirlpool flags a dead element as the F7E1 code ('heating element doesn't work') and F7E2 as an element that won't shut off. Before assuming the element, though, confirm the Heated Dry option is actually turned on and rinse aid is full — those are free. If the element or its high-limit thermostat has failed, replacement runs $180 to $460. $89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair."
  },
  {
    q: "How is Whirlpool drying different from Bosch?",
    a: "Fundamentally different, and it changes the fix. Bosch (and Miele) use condensation drying — no heating element, relying on a hot rinse and a cool tub — so on those there's nothing to 'burn out.' Whirlpool-family machines (including KitchenAid and Maytag) mostly use heated dry with an exposed calrod element, or a fan on some models. That means a Whirlpool that stops drying CAN have a failed element, thermostat, or fan, whereas a Bosch with the same symptom is almost always rinse aid, loading, or the dry option. Our general dishwasher not-drying page explains the full condensation-vs-heated-dry picture."
  },
  {
    q: "Is the Heated Dry (or Sani Rinse) option even turned on?",
    a: "Check this first — it's the most common non-repair reason a Whirlpool leaves dishes wet. Whirlpool machines have a Heated Dry option (and heavier or Sani/High-Temp cycles add heat), and if you've been running the normal or eco cycle with heated dry off, the dishes come out damp by design. Turn on Heated Dry, or run a hotter cycle, and test a load. If dishes still come out wet with heated dry on, then it's worth looking at the element, thermostat, or fan."
  },
  {
    q: "What's the high-limit thermostat and how does it cause wet dishes?",
    a: "The high-limit thermostat is a safety that cuts power to the heating element if the tub gets too hot. If it fails or trips prematurely, it can stop the element from running long enough to dry the load — so dishes come out wet even though the element itself is fine. It's an inexpensive part and a technician tests it alongside the element with a meter, because a wet-dish complaint on a heated-dry Whirlpool can be either one. Thermostat replacement runs $180 to $340."
  },
  {
    q: "My Whirlpool has a drying fan — could that be it?",
    a: "Some Whirlpool-family models use a fan (and a vent) to move moist air out and speed drying instead of, or alongside, the element. If that fan fails, drying degrades. Whirlpool groups vent/fan faults in the FA family (for example FAE3, a drying-fan failure). If your model has a fan-assisted dry and dishes are wet with the dry option on and rinse aid full, the fan or its vent is a suspect — a technician diagnosis, $220 to $420."
  },
  {
    q: "Do rinse aid and loading matter on a Whirlpool too?",
    a: "Yes, they matter on every brand, and they're worth checking before any repair: keep rinse aid full (it makes water sheet off), space the load so air moves, unload the bottom rack first so upper-rack water doesn't drip down, and accept that plastic dries worst because it holds no heat. Those universal factors are covered in full on our general dishwasher not-drying page — this Whirlpool page focuses on the parts that can actually fail on a heated-dry machine: the element, the thermostat, and the fan."
  },
  {
    q: "Does this apply to KitchenAid and Maytag?",
    a: "Yes. KitchenAid and Maytag dishwashers share the Whirlpool platform, including the heated-dry element and the F7/FA code families. So a KitchenAid or Maytag that won't dry has the same suspects — element, high-limit thermostat, fan, or the dry option being off — with parts from the same catalog families. We treat all three as one platform."
  },
  {
    q: "When is Whirlpool 'not drying' a repair versus a habit fix?",
    a: "Habit fixes clear a lot of these: turn on Heated Dry, refill rinse aid, run a hotter cycle, load and unload smarter. It's a genuine repair when the heating element is dead (F7E1, wet dishes plus cool water), the high-limit thermostat has failed, or a drying fan (FAE3) has quit. Those are bounded, affordable parts and almost always worth fixing. The $89 diagnostic tells you which side you're on, and it's waived if you approve the repair."
  },
  {
    q: "What's your warranty?",
    a: "90 days on SDAR labor and parts for the work we perform — separate from any Whirlpool factory warranty. Same component fails inside 90 days, we replace it free. BHGS #A49573, BBB Accredited Business, EPA 608 Universal certified #1346255700410. $89 residential diagnostic, waived when you approve the repair."
  }
];

