<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Answer first</p>
    <h2>Whirlpool dries with a heating element — so unlike a Bosch, a wet-dish complaint can be a real part failure.</h2>

    <p><strong>Most Whirlpool-family dishwashers use <span class="hl">heated dry</span>: an exposed metal element at the tub floor (some also use a fan). That's different from Bosch's condensation drying, and it matters — a Whirlpool that stops drying CAN have a failed heating element (code <span class="code">F7E1</span>), a tripped high-limit thermostat, or a dead drying fan (<span class="code">FAE3</span>). But first, the two free checks that fix most cases: is the <span class="hl">Heated Dry option turned on</span>, and is the rinse-aid dispenser full? $89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair. BHGS #A49573, EPA 608 Universal certified (#1346255700410), BBB Accredited.</strong></p>

    <p>The universal drying factors — rinse aid, loading, unloading order, why plastic never fully dries — apply to every brand, and we keep that full explainer on our <a href="/services/dishwasher-repair/not-drying/">general dishwasher not drying</a> page (it also lays out the condensation-vs-heated-dry split). This page is the Whirlpool layer: the parts that can actually fail on a heated-dry machine.</p>

    <p>Everything here applies to <strong>KitchenAid and Maytag</strong> too (same platform). Codes: <a href="/services/dishwasher-repair/whirlpool-error-codes/">Whirlpool dishwasher error codes</a>. Parent pillar: <a href="/services/dishwasher-repair/">dishwasher repair</a>.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Check first (free)</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Heated Dry on? Rinse aid full?</h2>
    <p>Before any part, two settings fix a large share of Whirlpool wet-dish complaints. <strong>Heated Dry:</strong> if you've been running the normal or eco cycle with heated dry off, dishes come out damp by design — turn on Heated Dry, or choose a hotter/Sani cycle that adds final-rinse heat, and test a load. <strong>Rinse aid:</strong> it lowers water's surface tension so it sheets off instead of clinging; a heated-dry machine still relies on it. Refill the dispenser and set a mid or high dose. If dishes are still wet with heated dry on and rinse aid full, then it's a part — read on.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Cause 1</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Heating element failure — F7E1.</h2>
    <p>This is the Whirlpool-specific repair. The exposed element at the tub floor heats the load so the final moisture evaporates; when it burns out, dishes stay wet and the wash water usually feels under-heated too. Whirlpool codes a dead element as <span class="code">F7E1</span> ("heating element doesn't work") and an element that won't shut off as F7E2. Our techs meter the element on-site to confirm it's open before quoting — no guessing. Element replacement runs $180 to $460. This is the cause a Bosch can't have (no element), which is exactly why the brand matters.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Cause 2</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">High-limit thermostat — the element is fine but shuts off early.</h2>
    <p>The high-limit thermostat is a safety that cuts power to the element if the tub overheats. If it fails or trips too soon, the element can't run long enough to dry the load, so dishes come out wet even though the element itself tests good. A technician checks the thermostat alongside the element with a meter, because a wet-dish complaint on a heated-dry Whirlpool can be either part. Thermostat replacement runs $180 to $340 — an inexpensive fix once it's identified.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Cause 3</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Drying fan / vent — FAE3 on fan-assisted models.</h2>
    <p>Some Whirlpool-family dishwashers use a fan and vent to push moist air out and speed drying, either alongside or instead of the element. If that fan fails, drying degrades even with everything else working. Whirlpool groups vent and fan faults in the FA family — <span class="code">FAE3</span> is a drying-fan failure. If your model has fan-assisted dry and dishes are wet with heated dry on and rinse aid full, the fan or its vent is the suspect — a technician diagnosis, $220 to $420.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">The universal factors</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Rinse aid, loading, plastic — same on every brand.</h2>
    <p>Even on a heated-dry Whirlpool, the universal drying factors still bite: keep rinse aid full, space the load so air and heat move, unload the bottom rack first so upper-rack water doesn't drip onto dried dishes, and accept that plastic dries worst because it holds no heat. If a repair isn't needed, these are usually the answer. The full brand-agnostic explainer — including the condensation-vs-heated-dry design split — is on our <a href="/services/dishwasher-repair/not-drying/">dishwasher not drying</a> page.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">DIY checklist</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Try these before you book a visit.</h2>
    <ol class="num-list">
      <li><strong>Turn on Heated Dry</strong> (or run a hotter / Sani cycle that adds heat).</li>
      <li><strong>Refill rinse aid</strong> and set a mid/high dose.</li>
      <li><strong>Unload the bottom rack first</strong> to avoid drip-down.</li>
      <li><strong>Space the load;</strong> angle cups and bowls so they don't hold water.</li>
      <li><strong>Crack the door</strong> open a few minutes at cycle end.</li>
      <li><strong>Accept plastic</strong> dries last — give it a shake.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Still wet with heated dry on and rinse aid full — especially if the wash water feels cool? That's the element (F7E1), thermostat, or fan (FAE3), and it's a service call.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Honest opinion</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Whirlpool drying repairs are cheap and almost always worth it.</h2>
    <p>The parts that fail drying on a Whirlpool — the heating element, the high-limit thermostat, a drying fan — are inexpensive and bounded, so a wet-dish fix is nearly always worth doing regardless of the machine's age. The only time we'd steer you toward replacement is if the drying fault shows up alongside a wash-pump or control-board failure on an old unit, where the total climbs toward a new machine. We give you that math at the door. $89 diagnostic, waived if you approve the repair.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Pricing</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Drying-repair costs in Southern California.</h2>
    <p class="lead">$89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair. No emergency surcharge — same $89 anytime.</p>
    <table class="price-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Repair</th><th>Typical cost</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Diagnostic</td><td>$89, waived with repair</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Heating element (F7E1)</td><td>$180 to $460</td></tr>
        <tr><td>High-limit thermostat</td><td>$180 to $340</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Drying fan / vent (FAE3)</td><td>$220 to $420</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Rinse-aid dispenser</td><td>$180 to $320</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Warranty</td><td>90 days parts and labor</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
